<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Justo José de Urquiza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a>–<a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Justo José de Urquiza was an Argentine general and politician, long governor of Entre Ríos. He broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a> and defeated him at Caseros in 1852, then sponsored the Constitution of 1853 and served as first constitutional president of the Argentine Confederation from 1854 to 1860. After withdrawing from the field at Pavón in 1861 he returned to Entre Ríos, where he was assassinated in 1870.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/justo-jose-de-urquiza.jpg" alt="Justo José de Urquiza" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Charles DeForest Fredricks · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daguerrotipo_de_Justo_Jos%C3%A9_de_Urquiza_(recorte).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Justo José de Urquiza y García was born in Entre Ríos on 18 October 1801, the son of José Narciso de Urquiza Álzaga, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castro-urdiales">Castro Urdiales</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and María Cándida García González, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. Like many other nineteenth-century Argentine patriots he was a freemason.</p><p>He was governor of Entre Ríos while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a> governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> with powers delegated by the other provinces. Rosas often offered his resignation as a political gesture, counting on its rejection. In 1851, resentful of the dominance of Buenos Aires, Urquiza accepted it and resumed for Entre Ríos the powers delegated to Rosas, and he demanded a national constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and the Uruguayan liberals, Urquiza raised the Grand Army and forced Manuel Oribe to capitulate in October 1851, ending the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>. He defeated Rosas at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caseros">Battle of Caseros</a> on 3 February 1852, and the provinces that had backed Rosas changed sides. In 1853 a constituent assembly adopted a constitution based chiefly on the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-alberdi">Juan Bautista Alberdi</a>, and Urquiza was elected president. He was inaugurated in March 1854, with Paraná as the provisional capital and the whole of Entre Ríos federalised.</p><p>His government encouraged public education and colonisation, and agricultural colonies drew European immigrants; Urquiza founded Colonia San José in 1857. The Confederation lacked money, and a Law of Differential Duties in 1856 aimed to shift trade away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, which had seceded. War broke out in 1859, and on 23 October Urquiza defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-mitre">Bartolomé Mitre</a> at Cepeda, where the Buenos Aires army lost 2,000 prisoners. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-san-jose-de-flores">Pact of San José de Flores</a>, signed on 11 November, brought Buenos Aires back into the Confederation, though some federalists, among them Ricardo López Jordán, thought it too generous.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The settlement was short-lived, and in September 1861 Urquiza met Mitre's army again. The battle was indecisive, but Urquiza withdrew from the field and left the victory to Mitre. He retired to San José Palace in Entre Ríos, where he ruled until he was assassinated at the age of 69, together with his sons Justo and Waldino, by followers of his rival Ricardo López Jordán.</p><p>He supported a liberal constitution favouring economic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>, and some see him as a classical liberal. Streets and parks across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> bear his name, as does a commuter railway line in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, the Urquiza Line.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Justo José de Urquiza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of the United States and by the ideas of Juan Bautista Alberdi, but the province of Buenos Aires refused to join and existed as a separate State of Buenos Aires, while the other provinces formed the Argentine Confederation under <b>Justo José de Urquiza</b>, with its capital at Paraná.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Justo José de Urquiza</b>'s rising in Entre Ríos and Brazil's intervention in May 1851 defeated Oribe at the price of treaties confirming Brazil's right to intervene in Uruguay.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Justo José de Urquiza</b> defeated him at Caseros in 1852, a congress adopted the Constitution of 1853, still in force, and the government sat at Paraná.</blockquote><a href="/state/argentine-confederation">Argentine Confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Justo José de Urquiza</b> overthrew Juan Manuel de Rosas at Caseros in 1852, he called a congress that would reduce Buenos Aires's control of customs revenue.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-buenos-aires">State of Buenos Aires</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entre-rios-province">Entre Ríos Province</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. Died in San José palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-32.1,-59.3&amp;year=1845" aria-label="Entre Ríos Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Justo José de Urquiza"><image href="/maps/globes/g300s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5051 5180h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5051 5180h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-32.1,-59.3&amp;year=1845">What was here in 1845? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
